<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Onondaga Nation</h1><p class="st-meta">1450 CE – present (approx.) · capital Onondaga</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The central nation of the Iroquois Confederacy, keepers of the council fire and the wampum, whose territory near Syracuse still hosts the Grand Council.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Onondaga lived in the hills south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onondaga-lake">Onondaga Lake</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, near modern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syracuse">Syracuse</a>. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/iroquois-confederacy">Haudenosaunee</a> tradition says their chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tadodaho">Tadodaho</a> was the last and fiercest opponent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-peacemaker">the Peacemaker</a>, and that when he accepted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-law-of-peace">Great Law</a>, his nation was made keeper of the central fire, with Tadodaho as the title of the presiding chief.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Fourteen Onondaga chiefs sat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-council-qing">Grand Council</a>, more than any other nation, and the Onondaga kept the wampum belts that recorded treaties and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-law-of-peace">Great Law</a>. Their main town moved every few decades as soils and firewood were used up. Seventeenth-century leaders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garakontie">Garakontié</a> pursued diplomacy with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/new-france">New France</a>, which planted the short-lived <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> mission of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sainte-marie-de-gannentaha">Sainte-Marie de Gannentaha</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onondaga-lake">Onondaga Lake</a> in 1656.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>In 1779 an American expedition burned the main Onondaga town, and many Onondaga moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-river-ontario">Grand River</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> they kept a small territory, which remains under traditional chiefs, issues its own passports and in 2005 sued to recover its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homeland</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidney-hill">Sidney Hill</a> is the current <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tadodaho">Tadodaho</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/onondaga-nation.jpg" alt="Onondaga Nation" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: UN Biodiversity · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:22dec06-COP15-opening-ceremony-5714_(52548203403).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1450 CE – present (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Onondaga</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Garakontié, Sidney Hill</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Haudenosaunee traditional religion, Longhouse religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Onondaga</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Onoda'gega', Onondaga</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1738&amp;focus=onondaga-nation" aria-label="Onondaga Nation on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2840 652 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Onondaga Nation lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3160 908L3160 908"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3160 908h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3160 908h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3160 908h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Onondaga. <a href="/atlas?year=1738&amp;focus=onondaga-nation">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

